<?xml version="1.0"?>
<ScenContainer xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xmlns:xsd="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema">
  <ScenTitle>Pacific Fury #1, Bolt from the Blue, 1994</ScenTitle>
  <ScenDescription>&lt;BODY scroll=auto&gt;&lt;P&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;Pacific Fury 1 Bolt from the Blue! 1994&lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;13 Feb 1994.&lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;This is the first scenario of a 16 scenario campaign. Pacific Fury is very closely linked to the Northern Fury campaign. You can explore the situation on the &lt;A href="http://northernfury.bhgdesigns.com/index.php"&gt;webpage&lt;/A&gt; read the Northern Fury background document for further insight. The key nuggets&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;UL&gt;
&lt;LI&gt;Cold War continued, no thaw in relations. Germany is unified but somewhat unstable, the Warsaw Pact (WP) still active but somewhat less of a force than it was in the 1980s&lt;/LI&gt;
&lt;LI&gt;Two days ago, 11 Feb 1994 there was a bloody coup in Warsaw. Hard liners seized power from the struggling reformist Solidarity government. There has been little news but it seems Soviet troops have been called in to assist in stabilizing the country.&lt;/LI&gt;
&lt;LI&gt;Yesterday, 12 Feb 1994, the Winter Olympics in Lillehammer, opened with ominous indications of trouble brewing. Soviet and Eastern Bloc countries were thinly represented at the opening ceremonies and many of their athletes were not present at the start of the actual events this morning. The Soviet Olympic village was empty and initial investigation found that many of the athletes had simply disappeared, while only a few had flown home. Norway ordered the IOC to cancel the games and evacuate the Olympic Village.&lt;/LI&gt;
&lt;LI&gt;Japan, South Korea and Taiwan are moving to a heightened alert status and tensions are generally high. The Soviet Far East Command has increased exercise tempo and scale, nothing overt or threating but noticeable.&lt;/LI&gt;
&lt;LI&gt;Both China and North Korea have been extremely quiet over the past few weeks, oddly ratcheting back the typical rhetoric and down-scaling military exercises.&lt;/LI&gt;
&lt;LI&gt;It is now late evening (1200Z) on 13 Feb 1994.&lt;/LI&gt;&lt;/UL&gt;
&lt;P&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;&lt;U&gt;You are the commander of the USS Independence Carrier Battle Group&lt;/U&gt;&lt;/STRONG&gt;. Your carrier and its battle group are the US Navy's ‘Forward Deployed’ force based in Yokosuka Japan as part of the US 7&lt;SUP&gt;th&lt;/SUP&gt; Fleet. In a normal year, your force would spend the winter months in port refitting, but this is not a normal year. Instead, the battle group deployed to the Philippines in early January for a multi-carrier exercise with &lt;EM&gt;USS Constellation&lt;/EM&gt; and &lt;EM&gt;USS Abraham Lincoln&lt;/EM&gt;, then a Freedom of Navigation run through the Strait of Taiwan, and finally an amphibious exercise with the South Koreans.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;Now, plans to head home were canceled last night and we’ve been ordered to deploy to the south coast of Hokkaido for a deterrence operation. The Japanese government has been getting extremely nervous over the past few weeks and the fiasco at the Olympics has spooked them. We do a restricted passage drill through the Tsugaru Strait and spend about 72 hours patrolling up as far as the southern Kurils. The 35&lt;SUP&gt;th&lt;/SUP&gt; Fighter Wing at Misawa has promised us a squadron of F-16s to run some air defence drills as well.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;Should be a quiet week. What could possibly go wrong?&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;Designer’s notes&lt;/STRONG&gt;:&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;UL&gt;
&lt;LI&gt;This is a complex scenario. The Japanese Self Defence force is operating in the same battlespace you are but not under your control. They share the same airfields as well so ensure you understand which aircraft are under your command.&lt;/LI&gt;
&lt;LI&gt;You should turn 'special messages' ON, there is quite a bit of information fed to you during the scenario, and you will want to see it. Later in the scenario updates will give you a better idea on what is going on around the world and will give you an idea where subsequent scenarios will lead.&lt;/LI&gt;
&lt;LI&gt;Please respect the initial ready times for aircraft, this is to replicate the transition from routine operations to mobilization and general maintenance.&lt;/LI&gt;
&lt;LI&gt;Points are allotted or lost for destruction of air and sea forces as well as completion of tasked missions.&lt;/LI&gt;&lt;/UL&gt;&lt;/BODY&gt;</ScenDescription>
